window.COURSE_INDEX=[{"title":"Technical Art course wiki","summary":"Follow the course by week, revisit a mechanism, or find a practical check for an asset you are investigating.","slug":"index","group":"Course","text":"\r\n   Start with the current work\r\n\r\n  Week 2 connects asset data to an observable result.   Begin with [Game Assets, Pipelines, and Validation](week02.html), then use the [museum investigation guide](museum-lab.html) during the lab. The [exhibit guide](exhibit.html) helps you turn a small technical relationship into something a visitor can understand.\r\n\r\n  Observe  →  Inspect  →  Test  →  Verify  →  Explain  \r\n\r\nYou can contribute a useful question before you know the answer. Describe what should happen, what you actually observe, and what evidence could distinguish the possible causes.\r\n\r\n   Weekly route\r\n\r\n  Week   Focus   Available here  \r\n --- --- --- \r\n  [1](week01.html)   Technical Art foundations   Four lenses, observation, and investigation  \r\n  [2](week02.html)   Game assets, pipelines, and validation   Study guide, topic explanations, museum prompts  \r\n  [3](week03.html)   Python and automation fundamentals   Preview: turn a known check into a small report  \r\n  [4](week04.html)   Editor tool development   Planned syllabus outline  \r\n  [5](week05.html)   Applied math for Technical Artists   Planned syllabus outline  \r\n  [6](week06.html)   Introduction to Technical Animation   Planned syllabus outline  \r\n  [7](week07.html)   Midterm feedback and reflection   Planned syllabus outline  \r\n  [8](week08.html)   Real-time rendering pipeline   Planned syllabus outline  \r\n  [9](week09.html)   Shaders and material systems   Planned syllabus outline  \r\n  [10](week10.html)   Profiling and optimization   Planned syllabus outline  \r\n  [11](week11.html)   Procedural content and scalability   Planned syllabus outline  \r\n  [12](week12.html)   Production systems and maintainability   Planned syllabus outline  \r\n\r\nThe week sequence follows the Fall 2026 syllabus. This wiki does not set assignment deadlines or replace the current Canvas briefs.\r\n\r\n   Find the mechanism\r\n\r\n- A surface looks different: [materials and shaders](materials.html), [normals](normals.html), [color and numerical data](textures.html).\r\n- A pattern is mirrored or stretched: [UVs and texture lookup](uvs.html).\r\n- An import differs from Maya: [DCC-to-Unreal handoffs](handoff.html).\r\n- An object bends incorrectly: [skeletal assets](skeletal.html).\r\n- An object changes with distance: [mips, mesh LOD, and streaming](representations.html).\r\n- A fix is hard to explain or reproduce: [validation](validation.html) and [technical documentation](documentation.html).\r\n\r\n   Learn at two depths\r\n\r\nThe main explanation and inspection prompts establish the shared foundation. “Explore further” links open the mathematical, rendering, or implementation detail when you are ready. You do not need to finish every linked resource before using the lab guide.\r\n\r\nStart with the [glossary](glossary.html) when a term is unfamiliar, or use the [reading and visual library](resources.html) to choose a focused next resource.\r\n"},{"title":"Maintaining the course wiki","summary":"Add a week without duplicating explanations, losing evidence, or mixing a proposal with a confirmed course requirement.","slug":"maintaining","group":"Course","text":"\r\n   Add a week\r\n\r\n1. Copy the week template from the  templates  folder into  content .\r\n2. Set the title, group, order, status, summary, and updated date in the short metadata block.\r\n3. State the connection to earlier work and the action students should be able to perform.\r\n4. Link existing topic pages; add a new topic only when the mechanism needs its own explanation.\r\n5. Add an example, inspection question, relevant evidence, and a focused source.\r\n6. Build the wiki and check the new page's links.\r\n\r\nKeep published HTML beside the Markdown source so the wiki can be read directly from the workspace. There is no server requirement for reading or searching the built pages.\r\n\r\n   A reusable teaching structure\r\n\r\nBegin with a visible problem or intended result. Explain the minimum mechanism needed to reason about it. Show where that mechanism can be inspected in Maya or Unreal, then ask a prediction or diagnosis question.\r\n\r\nUse the lab to motivate the explanation. Leave a breadcrumb back to the relevant topic page, while keeping the full instructor repair key in the course's instructor materials.\r\n\r\nPut advanced mathematics and implementation under a focused further-reading question. A later week can deepen an existing topic page rather than replacing its beginner explanation.\r\n\r\n   Evidence and sources\r\n\r\nDistinguish a real course capture, a publisher's example, and a proposed recording. Preserve creator credit, the source page, and useful version context. A conceptual citation does not prove that a separate image depicts the same technique or that a local repair succeeded.\r\n\r\nRecord enough context for a setting to remain meaningful after the software changes: what it controls, the importer or workflow, and the result being verified.\r\n\r\n   Revision habits\r\n\r\nUse the changelog to record the substantive change and reason. Preserve stable page filenames and current slide-number anchors. When a title changes, update navigation and references while retaining a clear path from the previous review.\r\n\r\nKeep current content, proposed changes, visual design, teaching rationale, and read-aloud notes distinct in the [slide reviews](../Slides/index.html). The review is an iterative proposal until the deck is actually edited.\r\n\r\n   Course policy and readiness\r\n\r\nLabel future weeks as planned until their teaching material is developed. Use the current Canvas brief for assignment scope, deadlines, grading, and submission. The instructor's current exhibit direction takes precedence over the older Week 2 assignment name in the syllabus snapshot.\r\n\r\nMuseum station availability comes from a rehearsed teaching build and prepared station list. A source-ready or display-only label does not establish a supported student repair task.\r\n\r\n   Build and source control\r\n\r\nThe repository [README](README.md) documents build commands, source locations, and the dependency version. Add the Markdown, generated HTML, and referenced local assets together in Perforce so a checked-out copy remains usable. Keep caches,  node_modules , and editor lock files out of the changelist.\r\n"},{"title":"Week 1 · Technical Art foundations","summary":"Turn a production problem into an investigation that another person can understand and continue.","slug":"week01","group":"Weeks","text":"\r\n   What carries forward\r\n\r\nTechnical Art connects artistic intention with the tools, data, and production conditions needed to achieve it. Sometimes the useful intervention is code. It can also be a clearer standard, a better handoff, a reproducible test, or an explanation that prevents repeated confusion.\r\n\r\nBefore choosing a solution, identify the intended experience. An attractive screenshot is only one condition: the asset may also need to survive animation, reimport, another camera, or another person's changes.\r\n\r\n   The four lenses\r\n\r\n  Lens   Question to ask   Asset-pipeline example  \r\n --- --- --- \r\n  Constraints   What limits the acceptable solution?   The asset needs to remain readable from the intended camera.  \r\n  Systems   Which people, tools, assets, and dependencies connect?   Maya assignments, exported identities, UE slots, and materials all participate.  \r\n  Leverage   Could the intervention help repeated work?   A known import check could become a reusable validator.  \r\n  Throughput   Does the whole workflow produce reliable work more efficiently?   A quicker export is unhelpful if every import needs manual repair.  \r\n\r\nUse the lenses to develop the question. Naming all four without applying them does not explain the production problem.\r\n\r\n   Observation before explanation\r\n\r\n“The label reads backward on this panel” is an observation. “The texture is broken” is a proposed explanation. A mirrored image, UV layout, or material-coordinate operation could produce similar visible results.\r\n\r\n1. State the intended result.\r\n2. Record the actual symptom and conditions.\r\n3. Propose an explanation and a plausible alternative.\r\n4. Choose evidence that would separate them.\r\n5. Describe what remains uncertain.\r\n\r\nThis is useful work even when you cannot yet perform the repair.\r\n\r\n   A small retrieval exercise\r\n\r\nChoose one visible feature in a game or a provided production case. Describe it without guessing its implementation. Apply two lenses to explain why it matters, then identify the first source, measurement, or inspection you would seek.\r\n\r\nFor a shading difference, would you inspect geometry first, the texture role, the material connection, or the point where the result first diverges between tools? Explain what your choice could establish.\r\n\r\n   Connection to Week 2\r\n\r\n[Week 2](week02.html) supplies specific data to inspect: geometry, normals, UVs, textures, assignments, and import settings. The investigation habit remains the same. [Week 3](week03.html) will turn a well-understood repeated check into a small program.\r\n\r\n   Course grounding\r\n\r\nThis guide summarizes the existing Week 1 student deck: shared vocabulary at slides 78–79, the four lenses at 81–89, the normal-map pipeline case at 130, and observation guidance at 134. The original Canvas journal prompt remains the authority for that activity's formal requirements.\r\n"},{"title":"Week 2 · Assets, pipelines, and validation","summary":"Follow a surface from Maya to Unreal, identify the data behind its appearance, and investigate a museum example with evidence.","slug":"week02","group":"Weeks","text":"\r\n   The common goal\r\n\r\nYou should be able to locate relevant asset data, explain a useful test, and document what the result establishes. You are not expected to master every modeling, rendering, or rigging technique in one lecture.\r\n\r\nWe begin with the parts of an asset, follow a DCC-to-engine handoff, and observe the museum. The focused anatomy examples then give you mechanisms to use in the repair investigation.\r\n\r\n   A map of the relationships\r\n\r\n  Part   Its role   First useful question  \r\n --- --- --- \r\n  [Mesh](meshes.html)   Positions and connections define the surface.   Did the actual shape or structure change?  \r\n  [Normals](normals.html)   Directions influence the shading response.   Which direction data is being used?  \r\n  [UVs](uvs.html)   Coordinates guide a texture lookup.   Where are we reading the image?  \r\n  [Texture](textures.html)   Stored values provide sampled inputs.   What do the channels mean?  \r\n  [Material](materials.html)   Logic and settings describe surface behavior.   Which input or operation controls this difference?  \r\n  Assignment   Associates a material with a mesh region or slot.   Is the intended region using the intended material?  \r\n  [Import configuration](handoff.html)   Controls how a representation becomes a destination asset.   What was preserved, converted, or generated?  \r\n\r\nThe rendering system combines asset information with scene and viewing conditions to produce the displayed image. Keep that system as one box for now. The useful task is to identify which input or relationship could explain the difference.\r\n\r\n   Follow one handoff\r\n\r\nIn Maya, inspect a texture connection and the faces assigned to its receiving material. At export, identify the mesh and attributes that need to travel. In Unreal, inspect the resulting mesh, material slots, and material or instance assignments.\r\n\r\nThen revise the source and reimport. Verify both the intended change and the relationships that should remain stable. This second delivery tests whether the bridge is maintainable. The [handoff guide](handoff.html) explains what to inspect without prescribing a universal preset.\r\n\r\n   Before the museum repair\r\n\r\nUse the first pass to gather observations. For each selected example, record the intended result, the difference, and a possible next test. An uncertain cause is a valid starting point.\r\n\r\nBefore changing a setting, use these concept prompts:\r\n\r\n-   Normal response:   did the outline change, or only the response to lighting? [Normal guide](normals.html).\r\n-   Color/data interpretation:   what does the input represent, and does its interpretation match that role? [Texture guide](textures.html).\r\n-   UV lookup:   is the source image correct, and which coordinates sample it? [UV guide](uvs.html).\r\n-   Assignment/alpha:   which faces use the material, and how does the material use coverage data? [Material guide](materials.html).\r\n-   Distance changes:   which texture or mesh representation is active? [Representation guide](representations.html).\r\n\r\nThese prompts suggest inspections. They do not identify the cause of every similar-looking failure.\r\n\r\n   During the investigation\r\n\r\nUse the instructor's prepared station list and group-owned editable assets. Keep the reference visible, change one relevant variable at a time, and repeat the original test. A copied map alone may still reference shared textures and materials.\r\n\r\nThe [museum lab guide](museum-lab.html) gives the observation and evidence structure. The [documentation guide](documentation.html) shows how to leave a record that someone else can reproduce and reset.\r\n\r\n   After the lab\r\n\r\nFor your [own exhibit](exhibit.html), choose one mechanism that a visitor can observe. Build a controlled variation and a useful prediction question. More elaborate assets are valuable only when they make the explanation clearer.\r\n\r\nFor [Week 3](week03.html), identify one explicit rule: what information is needed, what state is expected, and what should be reported when the role is unknown?\r\n\r\n   Review questions\r\n\r\n1. Can a material work without an image texture? Explain an example.\r\n2. Why might a correct source image appear mirrored on a surface?\r\n3. What evidence would distinguish a normal-map effect from a geometry change?\r\n4. Why is a successful reimport a different test from the first import?\r\n5. What would another person need to repeat your verification?\r\n\r\n   Explore further\r\n\r\nUse the [reading library](resources.html) for specific chapters rather than reading entire rendering references at once. The core software examples are Maya and Unreal; the underlying questions about data, interpretation, and evidence transfer across tools.\r\n"},{"title":"Week 3 · Python, Maya automation, and artist-facing UI","summary":"Read a small Python operation, automate a scoped texel-density repair in Maya, and make its state, scope, and evidence understandable to an artist.","slug":"week03","group":"Weeks","text":"\n   The through-line\n\nWeek 2 established a manual   Get → Set → verify   texel-density repair. This week\nturns that understood operation into a small tool:\n\n1. inspect a reference and selected targets;\n2. measure world-space and UV area;\n3. calculate the required UV scale;\n4. change only the intended UV shells;\n5. measure again and report the result.\n\nPython repeats controlled steps. It does not choose the artistic reference, decide\nwhether the final texture treatment is convincing, or make missing evidence true.\n\n   What you should be able to do\n\nBy the end of class, you should be able to:\n\n- read a short loop, condition, function call, and returned value;\n- explain the difference between VS Code, a Python interpreter,  maya.cmds , and\n  PySide;\n- identify the reference, targets, units, and protected scope of an operation;\n- distinguish   Check   from   Apply   and successful execution from verified output;\n- trace a button click through a callback into shared Maya code;\n- name one artist decision that should remain outside the automation.\n\n   Before changing a scene\n\nState the tool contract in plain language:\n\n-   Input:   one captured reference, one or more selected target meshes, map size,\n  active UV set, and current Maya linear unit.\n-   Action:   uniformly scale each existing UV shell around its own center.\n-   Protected scope:   the reference and unselected meshes are not edited.\n-   Evidence:   report before and after density and verify the result within tolerance.\n-   Recovery:   one Apply to multiple targets is one Maya undo step.\n\nThe classroom tool does not remap, pack, cut, sew, define a UDIM policy, or change\nmaterial tiling.\n\n   The systems-thinking layer\n\nTreat the tool as a small system, not an isolated button:\n\n-   Boundary:   pure Python calculates; Maya supplies and changes scene data; PySide\n  communicates with the artist; the filesystem supplies code and presets.\n-   State and context:   selection order, active UV set, scene unit, map size, project\n  path, and Maya/Python/PySide version all affect behavior.\n-   Feedback loop:   inspect → decide → change → measure again. Running Apply twice\n  tests whether the system stabilizes instead of drifting.\n\nThe highest-leverage choices occur at boundaries: validate before mutation, expose\ncontext instead of assuming it, protect scope, make the action undoable, and verify\nthe output. If the UI says “Done” while an invalid target was skipped, the software\nran but the system failed to communicate truthfully.\n\n   Interactive start\n\nUse [futurecoder](https://futurecoder.io/) for beginner Python practice and immediate\nfeedback. In class, the route is deliberately short: predict, run, read the feedback,\nand explain one change. Students who need support can use hints or a Parsons-style\nreordering problem; students who finish early should construct a counterexample to a\nwrong condition.\n\nThe local [Week 3 interactive bridge](../Slides/Week03/Interactive/index.html) uses\nthe actual workshop data and remains available when the hosted course is not. It\nconnects code prediction to loop tracing, counterexamples, and UI critique.\n\nThe first checkpoint asks you to predict this result before running it:\n\n   python\ndef needs_attention(name, density, minimum=9.75, maximum=10.75):\n    density_is_wrong = not minimum \u003c= density \u003c= maximum\n    name_is_wrong = not name.startswith(\"TD_\")\n    return density_is_wrong or name_is_wrong\n\nassets = [\n    (\"TD_reference_cube\", 10.24),\n    (\"TD_target_large_cube\", 4.10),\n    (\"prop_round_mesh\", 6.80),\n]\n\nprint([name for name, density in assets if needs_attention(name, density)])\n   \n\nChange only one part of the condition, then construct the smallest example that shows\nwhy the changed rule is wrong.\n\n   Maya checkpoint sequence\n\nThe workshop is staged rather than live-typed as one large script:\n\n1. Discover the project root and report the actual software environment.\n2. Read and predict an ordinary Python loop.\n3. Ask Maya for selection and polygon evidence.\n4. Measure density without changing the scene.\n5. Apply the reference density, verify, run twice, and undo.\n6. Operate a deliberately unclear but safe UI.\n7. Improve one artist-facing behavior and rerun the same test.\n\nThe supplied adapter contains the mesh API details. Introductory work stays focused on\nthe readable orchestration: inputs, condition, function call, result, and feedback.\n\n   UI/UX is part of correctness\n\nAn artist should not have to guess:\n\n- what “Value” means or what units it uses;\n- which objects a button will affect;\n- whether the action only checked or actually changed the scene;\n- which objects changed, failed, or were skipped;\n- whether the result was measured after the change;\n- how to recover from an unwanted action.\n\nThe completed interface therefore names the reference and target scope, distinguishes\nCheck from Apply, prevents Apply until a reference is captured, reports each verified\nresult or skip, and names Undo as the recovery route.\n\n   Pair lab\n\nOne person operates while the other observes silently. Before editing code, write one\nexpected behavior. Improve one label, validation response, callback, or report; then\nrun the same fixture test, Apply twice, confirm the protected control is unchanged,\nand switch roles.\n\nThere is no Week 3 submission. Further practice is optional and can become evidence\nfor choosing a useful midterm tool idea.\n"},{"title":"Week 4 · Editor tool development","summary":"Turn a useful check into an interface that an artist can operate and understand.","slug":"week04","group":"Weeks","text":"\r\n   Connection to the course\r\n\r\nReuse the separation between collecting data, evaluating a rule, and reporting a result from Week 3. A control should expose a meaningful choice; a report should identify the affected asset and explain the outcome.\r\n\r\n   Questions to carry forward\r\n\r\n- What does the user need to know before running the check?\r\n- How should a missing selection or unknown asset role appear?\r\n- What evidence would show that the tool improves the existing workflow?\r\n\r\n   Prepare with existing material\r\n\r\nRevisit the [related foundation](week03.html) and choose one example whose assumptions or evidence you can explain. The [glossary](glossary.html) and [reading library](resources.html) provide a starting point when a term is unfamiliar.\r\n\r\n   Development status\r\n\r\nThe topic follows the Fall 2026 syllabus. These guiding questions are a first course-wiki outline, not a finalized lesson, exercise, or assessment brief. Add the actual lecture examples and approved requirements as the week is developed. Use the current Canvas pages for formal course instructions.\r\n"},{"title":"Week 5 · Applied math for Technical Artists","summary":"Use mathematical relationships to explain concrete changes in position, orientation, and surface behavior.","slug":"week05","group":"Weeks","text":"\r\n   Connection to the course\r\n\r\nReturn to the normal direction and UV-coordinate examples from Week 2. Start with a visible operation and its coordinate frame before extending the representation. A calculation becomes useful when its inputs and expected effect are clear.\r\n\r\n   Questions to carry forward\r\n\r\n- Which coordinate frame are these values expressed in?\r\n- Is this quantity a position, a direction, or a scalar?\r\n- What simple case would let you check the result by inspection?\r\n\r\n   Prepare with existing material\r\n\r\nRevisit the [related foundation](normals.html) and choose one example whose assumptions or evidence you can explain. The [glossary](glossary.html) and [reading library](resources.html) provide a starting point when a term is unfamiliar.\r\n\r\n   Development status\r\n\r\nThe topic follows the Fall 2026 syllabus. These guiding questions are a first course-wiki outline, not a finalized lesson, exercise, or assessment brief. Add the actual lecture examples and approved requirements as the week is developed. Use the current Canvas pages for formal course instructions.\r\n"},{"title":"Week 6 · Introduction to Technical Animation","summary":"Connect motion and deformation to the asset relationships that make them work.","slug":"week06","group":"Weeks","text":"\r\n   Connection to the course\r\n\r\nDeepen the Week 2 distinction between joint pose, surface following, and playback. Follow one deformation through its source, exported data, and destination behavior before broadening to more complex animation systems.\r\n\r\n   Questions to carry forward\r\n\r\n- Does the problem appear in the joint motion or the surface following?\r\n- Which reference relationships and dependencies must survive the handoff?\r\n- How will you test the complete required motion rather than a convenient pose?\r\n\r\n   Prepare with existing material\r\n\r\nRevisit the [related foundation](skeletal.html) and choose one example whose assumptions or evidence you can explain. The [glossary](glossary.html) and [reading library](resources.html) provide a starting point when a term is unfamiliar.\r\n\r\n   Development status\r\n\r\nThe topic follows the Fall 2026 syllabus. These guiding questions are a first course-wiki outline, not a finalized lesson, exercise, or assessment brief. Add the actual lecture examples and approved requirements as the week is developed. Use the current Canvas pages for formal course instructions.\r\n"},{"title":"Week 7 · Midterm feedback and reflection","summary":"Use evidence from the current work to choose the next improvement and explain what you learned.","slug":"week07","group":"Weeks","text":"\r\n   Connection to the course\r\n\r\nRevisit the four lenses and the intended user of the tool. A revision should respond to a specific observation, failure, or usability problem. Compare the result against the original need instead of only describing implementation effort.\r\n\r\n   Questions to carry forward\r\n\r\n- Which feedback identifies a reproducible problem?\r\n- What change offers the most useful improvement within the remaining constraints?\r\n- What evidence would demonstrate that the revision helped?\r\n\r\n   Prepare with existing material\r\n\r\nRevisit the [related foundation](week01.html) and choose one example whose assumptions or evidence you can explain. The [glossary](glossary.html) and [reading library](resources.html) provide a starting point when a term is unfamiliar.\r\n\r\n   Development status\r\n\r\nThe topic follows the Fall 2026 syllabus. These guiding questions are a first course-wiki outline, not a finalized lesson, exercise, or assessment brief. Add the actual lecture examples and approved requirements as the week is developed. Use the current Canvas pages for formal course instructions.\r\n"},{"title":"Week 8 · Real-time rendering pipeline","summary":"Open the rendering box that Week 2 deliberately kept simple.","slug":"week08","group":"Weeks","text":"\r\n   Connection to the course\r\n\r\nThe earlier material overview traced inputs and assignments to a displayed result. This week can examine the intermediate work and where a visible result acquires cost. Keep explanations connected to a concrete scene and observation.\r\n\r\n   Questions to carry forward\r\n\r\n- Which stage could explain the visible difference?\r\n- What changes when the camera, light, geometry, or material changes?\r\n- What evidence supports a cost hypothesis before optimization?\r\n\r\n   Prepare with existing material\r\n\r\nRevisit the [related foundation](materials.html) and choose one example whose assumptions or evidence you can explain. The [glossary](glossary.html) and [reading library](resources.html) provide a starting point when a term is unfamiliar.\r\n\r\n   Development status\r\n\r\nThe topic follows the Fall 2026 syllabus. These guiding questions are a first course-wiki outline, not a finalized lesson, exercise, or assessment brief. Add the actual lecture examples and approved requirements as the week is developed. Use the current Canvas pages for formal course instructions.\r\n"},{"title":"Week 9 · Shaders and material systems","summary":"Develop surface behavior and useful artist controls with an understanding of the rendering work involved.","slug":"week09","group":"Weeks","text":"\r\n   Connection to the course\r\n\r\nExtend the Week 2 material graph, instance, and data-role model. The deeper question is how to design logic and controls that support the intended appearance across variations without hiding important assumptions.\r\n\r\n   Questions to carry forward\r\n\r\n- Which controls express an artistic intention clearly?\r\n- What inputs and coordinate conventions does the system assume?\r\n- How will the comparison distinguish an appearance improvement from an unrelated lighting change?\r\n\r\n   Prepare with existing material\r\n\r\nRevisit the [related foundation](materials.html) and choose one example whose assumptions or evidence you can explain. The [glossary](glossary.html) and [reading library](resources.html) provide a starting point when a term is unfamiliar.\r\n\r\n   Development status\r\n\r\nThe topic follows the Fall 2026 syllabus. These guiding questions are a first course-wiki outline, not a finalized lesson, exercise, or assessment brief. Add the actual lecture examples and approved requirements as the week is developed. Use the current Canvas pages for formal course instructions.\r\n"},{"title":"Week 10 · Profiling and optimization","summary":"Use measurements and controlled comparisons to evaluate quality and cost.","slug":"week10","group":"Weeks","text":"\r\n   Connection to the course\r\n\r\nReturn to the difference between a hypothesis and evidence. Inspect the actual bottleneck, choose a change with a reason, then compare the intended quality and measured behavior under consistent conditions.\r\n\r\n   Questions to carry forward\r\n\r\n- Is the proposed bottleneck supported by a measurement?\r\n- What viewing or workload conditions must remain comparable?\r\n- Did the change preserve the visual and interaction requirements?\r\n\r\n   Prepare with existing material\r\n\r\nRevisit the [related foundation](representations.html) and choose one example whose assumptions or evidence you can explain. The [glossary](glossary.html) and [reading library](resources.html) provide a starting point when a term is unfamiliar.\r\n\r\n   Development status\r\n\r\nThe topic follows the Fall 2026 syllabus. These guiding questions are a first course-wiki outline, not a finalized lesson, exercise, or assessment brief. Add the actual lecture examples and approved requirements as the week is developed. Use the current Canvas pages for formal course instructions.\r\n"},{"title":"Week 11 · Procedural content and scalability","summary":"Evaluate repeatable generation as a system with inputs, constraints, and artistic control.","slug":"week11","group":"Weeks","text":"\r\n   Connection to the course\r\n\r\nBuild on parameters, explicit data roles, validation, and reusable checks. A procedural result is useful when its controls produce understandable variations and its outputs can be tested against the intended use.\r\n\r\n   Questions to carry forward\r\n\r\n- Which inputs give the artist meaningful control?\r\n- Can the same recorded inputs reproduce the intended output?\r\n- What validation is needed when the system produces many assets?\r\n\r\n   Prepare with existing material\r\n\r\nRevisit the [related foundation](validation.html) and choose one example whose assumptions or evidence you can explain. The [glossary](glossary.html) and [reading library](resources.html) provide a starting point when a term is unfamiliar.\r\n\r\n   Development status\r\n\r\nThe topic follows the Fall 2026 syllabus. These guiding questions are a first course-wiki outline, not a finalized lesson, exercise, or assessment brief. Add the actual lecture examples and approved requirements as the week is developed. Use the current Canvas pages for formal course instructions.\r\n"},{"title":"Week 12 · Production systems and maintainability","summary":"Prepare a tool or asset system for other people to use, revise, and continue.","slug":"week12","group":"Weeks","text":"\r\n   Connection to the course\r\n\r\nReturn to the Maya-to-Unreal handoff and exhibit record. The first successful result is only one test. Source ownership, dependencies, reset behavior, and a clear explanation support the next delivery and the next collaborator.\r\n\r\n   Questions to carry forward\r\n\r\n- Can another person reproduce the intended starting state?\r\n- Which assumptions must be recorded or checked after a change?\r\n- How will you detect a regression and explain a known limitation?\r\n\r\n   Prepare with existing material\r\n\r\nRevisit the [related foundation](documentation.html) and choose one example whose assumptions or evidence you can explain. The [glossary](glossary.html) and [reading library](resources.html) provide a starting point when a term is unfamiliar.\r\n\r\n   Development status\r\n\r\nThe topic follows the Fall 2026 syllabus. These guiding questions are a first course-wiki outline, not a finalized lesson, exercise, or assessment brief. Add the actual lecture examples and approved requirements as the week is developed. Use the current Canvas pages for formal course instructions.\r\n"},{"title":"Mesh anatomy","summary":"Geometry, topology, normals, UVs, and assignments describe different parts of the same asset.","slug":"meshes","group":"Topics","text":"\r\n   Shape and structure\r\n\r\nA vertex has a position. Edges connect vertices. Faces use ordered boundaries to describe a surface.   Geometry   describes the shape;   topology   describes its connections. Moving a point changes the shape. Adding a face or splitting a connection changes the structure as well.\r\n\r\nThat structure can support different jobs: a static silhouette, a subdivision surface, a bake target, or a mesh that needs to bend. A useful modeling rule must account for the operation and intended result.\r\n\r\n   A mesh carries more than positions\r\n\r\n  Data   What it helps determine  \r\n --- --- \r\n  Positions and connectivity   Shape and structure  \r\n  Shading normals   How directions vary across the shaded surface  \r\n  UV coordinates   Where textures are sampled  \r\n  Material membership / slots   Which surface definition applies to a region  \r\n  Transform and pivot   How the object relates to its scene  \r\n\r\nTwo faces can meet at one editable point but need distinct UV or normal values at the boundary. Different applications may count different representations of a vertex. Ask what is being counted before treating a numerical difference as a failure. Autodesk's [polygon data-model explanation](https://help.autodesk.com/cloudhelp/2023/CHS/Maya-SDK/files/Polygon-API/Maya_SDK_Polygon_API_How_polygons_are_handled_html.html) is a useful deeper reference.\r\n\r\n   Inspect in Maya, verify in Unreal\r\n\r\nSelect the actual components around the problem. Compare the outline, wireframe, face orientation, and shading. An unusual surface appearance alone does not establish a topology problem.\r\n\r\nAfter a source edit and import, check the original symptom and the relationships that should survive: dimensions, UVs, material assignment, and required motion or interaction. See [Epic's static-mesh pipeline](https://dev.epicgames.com/documentation/unreal-engine/fbx-static-mesh-pipeline-in-unreal-engine?application_version=5.7) for the transfer context.\r\n\r\n   Avoid automatic conclusions\r\n\r\nAn open boundary can be intentional. Overlapping UVs may deliberately reuse a pattern. Generated normals can be a pipeline choice. Each becomes a problem when it conflicts with the asset's requirements or invalidates another dependency.\r\n\r\n  Try this:   compare a beveled edge with a shading-only change. Which effect remains visible in silhouette? Continue with [normals](normals.html), then [UV lookup](uvs.html).\r\n"},{"title":"Normals and normal maps","summary":"Distinguish face orientation, mesh shading directions, and sampled normal-map information before choosing a repair.","slug":"normals","group":"Topics","text":"\r\n   A normal is a direction\r\n\r\nA vector can describe a direction and magnitude. A face normal is perpendicular to the face. The directions used for shading may be authored or interpolated across the surface to control its appearance.\r\n\r\nFace orientation also depends on the ordered vertices around a face. The front/back relationship and the shading directions are related concepts, but they are different things to inspect. [Autodesk's polygon-normal guide](https://help.autodesk.com/cloudhelp/2027/ENU/Maya-Modeling/files/GUID-9C257D44-924D-4B3F-ADEF-C71FAA98EAB1.htm) grounds the Maya workflow.\r\n\r\n   Hard and soft shading\r\n\r\nLook at two adjacent faces. A hard boundary can preserve a sharp shading transition; a soft boundary can allow a smoother transition. That shading change does not itself add a bevel or round the outline.\r\n\r\nIn Maya, compare hard/soft-edge behavior while keeping the mesh visible in wireframe. Predict whether the vertex positions changed, then check the silhouette.\r\n\r\n   What a normal map adds\r\n\r\nA normal map supplies direction information across the surface. It can create apparent fine detail in the lighting response while the mesh outline remains the same. In the ordinary normal-input example, it does not create physical grooves or change collision.\r\n\r\n[LearnOpenGL's direction illustration](https://learnopengl.com/img/advanced-lighting/normal_mapping_surfaces.png) and [brick comparison](https://learnopengl.com/img/advanced-lighting/normal_mapping_compare.png) are useful visual explanations. They are renderer-independent illustrations, not UE captures. The [full chapter](https://learnopengl.com/Advanced-Lighting/Normal-Mapping) explains tangent-space detail for further study.\r\n\r\n   A useful Unreal inspection\r\n\r\n1. Confirm which texture and material are actually used.\r\n2. Identify the intended normal-data convention and interpretation.\r\n3. Inspect the texture's compression/sampling configuration and the receiving material connection.\r\n4. Compare the result from more than one lighting direction.\r\n5. Check that geometry and unrelated surface inputs stayed as intended.\r\n\r\nUse the [Texture Asset Editor reference](https://dev.epicgames.com/documentation/en-us/unreal-engine/texture-asset-editor-in-unreal-engine?application_version=5.7) to locate the relevant settings. The visual color of a texture alone is not a pass/fail test for its interpretation.\r\n\r\n   A question for the museum\r\n\r\nIf a channel flip, compression change, and sampler change happen together, which one explained the result? Separate the presence of normal-map detail from whether the data is interpreted correctly. A suspected convention mismatch needs evidence from the actual pipeline.\r\n\r\nFor a deeper historical connection, Blinn's [Simulation of Wrinkled Surfaces](https://www.microsoft.com/en-us/research/publication/simulation-of-wrinkled-surfaces/) explains bump mapping. Treat it as research context, not a current Unreal import recipe.\r\n"},{"title":"UVs and texture lookup","summary":"The image stores values; coordinates determine where the surface reads them.","slug":"uvs","group":"Topics","text":"\r\n   Follow one sample\r\n\r\nA surface location needs coordinates for a texture lookup. In a common mesh workflow, UV coordinates provide a two-dimensional location in texture space. A texture sample uses the coordinates and filtering behavior to obtain values.\r\n\r\nThink about a label on a panel. The image can remain unchanged while the label appears mirrored because the coordinates changed. The material can also transform coordinates before sampling. These are different possible causes of a similar observation.\r\n\r\nUse the Maya figure in [Scratchapixel's Basic Implementation chapter](https://www.scratchapixel.com/lessons/3d-basic-rendering/introduction-to-texturing/introduction-to-texturing-basic-implementation.html) to follow the correspondence between a surface and an image region.\r\n\r\n   Inspect the correspondence\r\n\r\nIn Maya's [UV Editor](https://help.autodesk.com/cloudhelp/2027/ENU/Maya-Modeling/files/GUID-73BF7546-3BF2-44F3-9192-15A024CEC173.htm), select a surface region and locate its UVs. A directional checker with labeled corners is easier to interpret than a symmetric pattern.\r\n\r\nIn Unreal, identify the selected UV channel and any coordinate operations before the Texture Sample. The same image and mesh can produce different placement through the graph.\r\n\r\n   A small mathematical nugget\r\n\r\nWithin a simple normalized interval, replacing  U  with  1 − U  reverses the horizontal coordinate. At  U = 0.2 , the new value is  0.8 . This describes the sampling coordinate; it does not edit the pixels stored in the source image.\r\n\r\nOutside that simple example, wrapping, tiling, and other coordinate transforms affect the result. Begin with the labeled 0–1 case before extending it.\r\n\r\n   Layout choices have a purpose\r\n\r\nStretching, seams, orientation, and texel density affect how a pattern reads. An overlap is not automatically a defect: a repeated or symmetric surface may intentionally reuse data. Unique detail or a particular bake can impose a different requirement.\r\n\r\n  Try this:   keep the image fixed and mirror one UV region. Record what changed. Then restore it and perform the corresponding material-coordinate change in a prepared example. What evidence identifies which layer owns the difference?\r\n\r\nContinue with [textures](textures.html), [representations](representations.html), or the [museum prompts](museum-lab.html).\r\n"},{"title":"Texture data and interpretation","summary":"A texture stores channel values. Its intended role determines how those values should be interpreted and used.","slug":"textures","group":"Topics","text":"\r\n   An image is not always color information\r\n\r\nA texture might supply surface color, roughness, normal directions, or a mask. The channels can hold different kinds of information even when the asset looks like an ordinary image in a viewer.\r\n\r\nStart by identifying the declared role and the material connection. A grayscale appearance or filename can be a clue, but neither establishes the rule by itself.\r\n\r\n   Color and numerical control data\r\n\r\nColor encodings such as sRGB serve a different purpose from storing a numerical control signal for direct use. Applying a color-decoding transform to numerical RGB roughness data changes the numbers entering the material. A setting must agree with the intended role.\r\n\r\nThis does not mean every grayscale texture has the same configuration, or that every channel in a packed texture shares every interpretation rule. Inspect the actual channels and usage. Alpha, in particular, should not be casually assumed to receive the same treatment as RGB.\r\n\r\n[Epic's Texture Asset Editor documentation](https://dev.epicgames.com/documentation/en-us/unreal-engine/texture-asset-editor-in-unreal-engine?application_version=5.7) explains the available settings. Use the project configuration and its documented data roles to interpret them.\r\n\r\n   Separate the decisions\r\n\r\n  Decision   Question  \r\n --- --- \r\n  Role   What should these channels represent?  \r\n  Color interpretation   Is a decoding transform appropriate for those values?  \r\n  Compression   How is the data represented for the target use?  \r\n  Sampling   How does the graph read the data?  \r\n  Connection   Which material input receives the result?  \r\n  Display   What viewing transform helps us see the preview?  \r\n\r\nMaya's display/color-management configuration and an Unreal texture's sRGB setting occupy different parts of this process. Do not treat similar-looking previews as proof that their settings are equivalent.\r\n\r\n   A controlled comparison\r\n\r\nKeep geometry, camera, lighting, and Base Color fixed. Identify a known numerical roughness input. Compare one intended interpretation change and observe the surface response. If other settings also change, record them; you no longer have a one-variable test.\r\n\r\nFor your record, capture the role, expected setting, observed setting, result, and remaining limits. This is the bridge to [Week 3's Python check](week03.html).\r\n\r\n   Explore further\r\n\r\n[Adobe's PBR Guide, Part 2](https://www.adobe.com/learn/substance-3d-designer/web/the-pbr-guide-part-2) connects authoring choices with surface models. [Scratchapixel's texturing lessons](https://www.scratchapixel.com/lessons/3d-basic-rendering/introduction-to-texturing/introduction-to-texturing.html) provide the deeper sampling and color-space context. For size and distance behavior, continue with [mips and LOD](representations.html).\r\n"},{"title":"Materials and shaders","summary":"A shader performs rendering calculations; a material defines surface logic and settings; textures can supply some of its inputs.","slug":"materials","group":"Topics","text":"\r\n   Start with the roles\r\n\r\nA   shader   is a program used in rendering. It receives inputs, performs calculations, and produces outputs. In Unreal, you usually describe a surface through a   Material graph   and its settings. Unreal turns those descriptions into the rendering programs it needs.\r\n\r\nA   texture   supplies sampled values. A material can also use constants, adjustable parameters, or values calculated from coordinates and time. You can make a material using only a constant color and roughness value. An image file is optional.\r\n\r\n   Real Unreal editor imagery from Epic Games: a preview surface beside its graph. This is an Epic documentation example, not a capture of the course museum.  Source and context .  \r\n\r\n   The process is a set of relationships\r\n\r\n  Relationship   What to follow  \r\n --- --- \r\n  Coordinates guide sampling   UVs say where a texture sample reads.  \r\n  Values feed surface logic   Samples, constants, and parameters connect through operations to material inputs.  \r\n  Settings select behavior   Blend mode and other material settings govern how the surface participates in rendering.  \r\n  Assignment selects the surface definition   A mesh region uses a Material or Material Instance through its assignment.  \r\n  Rendering produces the image   The system uses the asset information with geometry, lighting, camera, and display processing.  \r\n\r\nKeep the final rendering stage as one box in Week 2. Texture pixels do not simply become part of a one-time shader-compilation step. The system still samples and uses data when it renders.\r\n\r\n   Recognize a few inputs\r\n\r\n-   Base Color:   supplies surface color information. It is not the final lit pixel.\r\n-   Roughness:   describes variation relevant to the width and sharpness of surface reflections. Compare it under fixed lighting.\r\n-   Normal:   supplies directions used in surface shading. See [normals](normals.html).\r\n-   Opacity / Opacity Mask:   participate in coverage or transparency through the selected material setup.\r\n\r\nThese are starting points. The available inputs depend on the chosen material configuration. Follow [Epic's Material Inputs reference](https://dev.epicgames.com/documentation/unreal-engine/material-inputs-in-unreal-engine?application_version=5.7) when inspecting a specific graph.\r\n\r\n   Material Instances\r\n\r\nA Material Instance inherits from a parent and can override exposed parameters. This gives an artist controls without duplicating every part of the graph. An ordinary color, scalar, or texture parameter is different from a static choice that can affect shader variants. See [Epic's instance guide](https://dev.epicgames.com/documentation/en-us/unreal-engine/instanced-materials-in-unreal-engine?application_version=5.7).\r\n\r\nTry predicting which object changes before editing an instance. Then inspect which asset each object's slot references. A shared material or instance can affect more than the selected actor.\r\n\r\n   Alpha is data; blend mode gives it a role\r\n\r\nAn alpha channel does not automatically make a surface transparent. The material must connect and interpret that data appropriately.\r\n\r\n  Mode   Beginner distinction  \r\n --- --- \r\n  Opaque   Draw an opaque surface.  \r\n  Masked   Keep or discard samples using a cutoff.  \r\n  Translucent   Support partial transparency through the chosen setup.  \r\n\r\n  Two Sided   is a separate backface-rendering decision. A visible cutout does not delete mesh triangles or collision. Compare the modes using the same geometry and image, then change only the mask cutoff. [Epic's blend-mode examples](https://dev.epicgames.com/documentation/en-us/unreal-engine/material-blend-modes-in-unreal-engine?application_version=5.7) show the relevant distinctions.\r\n\r\n   Lab questions\r\n\r\nIs the intended region using the correct material? Does the graph sample the intended texture and coordinates? Does the texture's role agree with its interpretation? Which single change would distinguish these possibilities?\r\n\r\nContinue with [Maya-to-Unreal handoffs](handoff.html) or [the reading library](resources.html) for shader implementation and physically based surface models.\r\n"},{"title":"Maya-to-Unreal handoffs","summary":"Follow identity, data, and dependencies through export, import, assignment, revision, and reimport.","slug":"handoff","group":"Topics","text":"\r\n   Begin in the source application\r\n\r\nIn Maya, a texture node can feed a material input. Material assignment associates that surface definition with an object or selected faces. UVs supply a separate relationship used for texture placement. Inspect each relationship before exporting.\r\n\r\nThe [Maya shading-network overview](https://help.autodesk.com/cloudhelp/2027/ENU/Maya-LightingShading/files/GUID-599CACDB-76C1-4FAC-AA94-0E77F0042C8D.htm) and [assignment documentation](https://help.autodesk.com/cloudhelp/2027/ENU/Maya-LightingShading/files/GUID-6BA1E3DF-F033-443C-989F-9A3295FFC152.htm) explain these roles.\r\n\r\n   What should survive?\r\n\r\nFor one asset with two material regions, write down the intended dimensions, regions, UVs, shading data, and texture dependencies. A handoff has succeeded only in relation to those requirements.\r\n\r\nArbitrary Maya or Arnold shading networks should not be assumed to translate intact through FBX. Supported material conversion and texture handling have limits, and the selected importer matters. A deliberate Unreal material setup may be needed even when mesh regions and slot identities arrive correctly. See [Epic's FBX Material Pipeline](https://dev.epicgames.com/documentation/en-us/unreal-engine/fbx-material-pipeline-in-unreal-engine?application_version=5.7).\r\n\r\n   Read settings by consequence\r\n\r\n  Area   What to establish before choosing an option  \r\n --- --- \r\n  Selection and hierarchy   Which objects and required relationships should travel?  \r\n  Scale, axes, transforms   What dimensions and orientation should appear in the destination?  \r\n  Normals and tangents   Is the pipeline preserving authored data or generating destination data?  \r\n  Materials and textures   Which assignments or supported inputs can transfer, and which are rebuilt?  \r\n  Animation/deformation   Which skeleton, skin, morph, and animation information does this asset require?  \r\n\r\nUse [Maya's FBX Export options](https://help.autodesk.com/cloudhelp/2027/ENU/Maya-Interoperability/files/GUID-FE8DBEAA-C2DD-43B3-9933-4BA4CDDEAA89.htm) and the reference for the importer you actually use: [FBX options](https://dev.epicgames.com/documentation/en-us/unreal-engine/fbx-import-options-reference-in-unreal-engine?application_version=5.7) or [Interchange](https://dev.epicgames.com/documentation/unreal-engine/interchange-import-reference-in-unreal-engine?application_version=5.7). Record the chosen path and software versions with your example.\r\n\r\n   Test the second delivery\r\n\r\n1. Inspect the source and record the intended state.\r\n2. Export and inspect the destination asset.\r\n3. Assign the appropriate Unreal Material or Instance to the intended regions.\r\n4. Revise one source relationship.\r\n5. Reimport and verify the revision.\r\n6. Check that unrelated assignments, scale, UVs, and behavior stayed correct.\r\n\r\nImport can generate data deliberately. Call that a failure when it violates the handoff requirement, not simply because a value was recomputed.\r\n\r\n   Maintenance is part of the asset\r\n\r\nRecord source ownership, dependencies, version assumptions, and verification. If the same problem returns after a source revision, a downstream patch may not have repaired the responsible layer.\r\n\r\nIn the lab, a material-interpretation fault may belong in Unreal; a source-geometry error may belong in Maya. Follow the evidence instead of forcing every repair through the same application. Continue with the [documentation example](documentation.html).\r\n"},{"title":"Skeletal assets","summary":"Separate the joint pose, the surface following that pose, and the animation being played.","slug":"skeletal","group":"Topics","text":"\r\n   The minimum useful model\r\n\r\nJoints form a hierarchy of transforms. Skin weights describe how vertices follow those transforms, and a bind/reference relationship connects the original mesh and joints. Animation changes the pose over time.\r\n\r\n“Weight” means a contribution to deformation, not the physical mass of a bone. Watch the same joint motion with different weights: the skeleton can move identically while the surface bends differently.\r\n\r\nThe Autodesk [Skin Tools demonstration, starting at 2:22](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=gJSkEyXkyHE&t=142s), shows weight editing and deformation testing in Maya 2027. Pair it with the [official quickstart](https://help.autodesk.com/cloudhelp/2027/ENU/Maya-CharacterAnimation/files/GUID-2ABD18C8-8CCF-44B9-99E0-EDEE4A56A3E6.htm).\r\n\r\n   Three inspection questions\r\n\r\n  Observation   Useful distinction  \r\n --- --- \r\n  The joints move unexpectedly.   Inspect the pose, hierarchy, and intended motion.  \r\n  The joints move correctly but the surface follows poorly.   Inspect weights, bind relationships, mesh structure, and the deformation method.  \r\n  The wrong motion appears or does not play.   Inspect the animation asset, compatibility, and playback configuration.  \r\n\r\nA rest pose does not verify a required bend or complete motion loop. The Unreal [Skeletal Mesh asset guide](https://dev.epicgames.com/documentation/unreal-engine/skeletal-mesh-assets-in-unreal-engine?application_version=5.7) explains the destination asset context.\r\n\r\n   A small mathematical picture\r\n\r\nFor a simplified linear-blend example, let  pA  and  pB  be candidate positions for the   same vertex   after the appropriate joint/bind transformations, expressed in the same coordinate frame. With two weights:\r\n\r\n p′ = 0.75 pA + 0.25 pB \r\n\r\nThese candidates are not the positions of the bones. The expression is a teaching model for combining contributions. Different deformation algorithms can produce different artifacts; [Kavan et al.'s skinning paper and comparisons](https://users.cs.utah.edu/~ladislav/kavan07skinning/kavan07skinning.html) provide an advanced continuation.\r\n\r\n   Morph targets and the lab\r\n\r\nMorph targets provide another form of shape change using corresponding mesh information. Keep that concept distinct from changing the skeleton's pose.\r\n\r\nUse skeletal museum examples as directed by the instructor's prepared station list. The current project documentation treats these as display examples; do not assume an available display is also a supported repair task. Full rigging and mathematical derivations return later in the course.\r\n"},{"title":"Mips, mesh LOD, and streaming","summary":"Several systems change what data is used at a distance. Identify which representation changed before explaining the result.","slug":"representations","group":"Topics","text":"\r\n   Three different questions\r\n\r\n  Concept   Main question  \r\n --- --- \r\n  Texture mip   Which lower-resolution representation of the texture is sampled?  \r\n  Mesh LOD   Which geometry representation is active?  \r\n  Streaming   Which required data is currently available in memory?  \r\n\r\nInstancing is another idea: it reuses a mesh representation for multiple instances. It should not be taught as another name for selecting a lower-detail mesh.\r\n\r\n   Why a smaller texture representation can help\r\n\r\nA detailed pattern can cover less than a screen pixel. Sampling needs to account for that footprint; simply using the highest-resolution source everywhere does not guarantee a stable or faithful distant image.\r\n\r\nUse a fixed camera path to observe shimmer, then pause on a comparable frame. The [Texture Asset Editor reference](https://dev.epicgames.com/documentation/en-us/unreal-engine/texture-asset-editor-in-unreal-engine?application_version=5.7) grounds the settings. Williams's [Pyramidal Parametrics, page 4](https://users.cs.northwestern.edu/~ago820/cs395/Papers/Williams_1983.pdf page=4) gives historical visual context for minification.\r\n\r\n   Why geometry may change\r\n\r\nA conventional static-mesh LOD setup can select a representation based on projected screen size. Inspect the active LOD and statistics while moving the camera, then check the silhouette and material behavior. [Epic's automatic LOD guide](https://dev.epicgames.com/documentation/en-us/unreal-engine/static-mesh-automatic-lod-generation-in-unreal-engine?application_version=5.7) explains that workflow.\r\n\r\nThe lower-detail result should preserve what matters under the intended viewing conditions. A numerical reduction alone does not establish an acceptable asset.\r\n\r\n   A lab comparison\r\n\r\nIf an object suddenly looks different at a distance, record the camera condition, active mesh representation, texture representation, and relevant residency information. Change one system at a time in a prepared example.\r\n\r\nThe same symptom can have several causes. Do not collapse a texture-mip demonstration, geometry-LOD demonstration, and streaming delay into one unexplained “quality” setting.\r\n\r\nFor more depth, PBRT's [Image Texture chapter](https://www.pbr-book.org/4ed/Textures_and_Materials/Image_Texture) discusses filtering footprints. Its implementation context is a further reading, not a set of UE editor instructions.\r\n"},{"title":"Validation and evidence","summary":"A useful check compares observed data with an explicit requirement and explains the scope of its result.","slug":"validation","group":"Topics","text":"\r\n   Start with an intended result\r\n\r\n“Looks wrong” identifies a reason to investigate. A useful test adds the expected behavior, the observed difference, and the conditions under which the difference appears.\r\n\r\nFor a readable label, the viewing direction and coordinate orientation matter. For a bend, the required range of motion matters. For a texture setting, the intended data role matters. A rule is meaningful because it protects one of these requirements.\r\n\r\n   Separate evidence from confidence\r\n\r\n  Result   What it establishes  \r\n --- --- \r\n  PASS   The available evidence satisfies this specific rule under the tested conditions.  \r\n  FAIL   The observed state conflicts with the explicit rule.  \r\n  REVIEW   The role, data, applicability, or evidence is insufficient for an automatic conclusion.  \r\n\r\nThese are suggested teaching/report labels. The real tool or exercise can define its own result vocabulary. Passing one rule does not prove complete artistic quality or correctness.\r\n\r\n   Make the comparison useful\r\n\r\nReproduce the original symptom. State a hypothesis and an alternative. Keep relevant viewing conditions fixed, change a variable that tests the hypothesis, and repeat the original check. Record any additional changes required by the tool.\r\n\r\nWhat observation would make you reject your explanation? If the answer is “nothing,” the proposed test probably cannot establish the cause.\r\n\r\n   Design a small checker\r\n\r\nBefore writing code, prepare a known passing example, a known failure, and an incomplete-information example. Decide which data must be declared and which can be measured.\r\n\r\n[Week 3's Python preview](week03.html) demonstrates this with in-memory texture records. A mature external example is the [Khronos glTF Validator](https://github.com/KhronosGroup/glTF-Validator): use it to study how validation produces specific findings, while recognizing that its format rules are not a course-wide UE asset policy.\r\n\r\n   Leave a usable record\r\n\r\nCapture the asset identity, rule, observed value, result, and reason. Link the relevant source and describe the verification limits. A precise unresolved diagnosis can save the next person substantial investigation time. See the [documentation guide](documentation.html).\r\n"},{"title":"Technical documentation","summary":"Help another person understand the intended result, reproduce the test, verify the outcome, and maintain the example.","slug":"documentation","group":"Topics","text":"\r\n   Two readers, one exhibit\r\n\r\nThe visitor needs to understand the phenomenon. The maintainer needs enough identity, procedure, and evidence to reproduce and reset it. A short explanation and a technical record can serve those purposes while agreeing about what the exhibit demonstrates.\r\n\r\nDocumentation earns its length by answering a reader's question. A list of every action you performed is less useful when it hides the tested claim and decisive evidence.\r\n\r\n   A published example\r\n\r\nThe Khronos AlphaBlendModeTest documentation shows an expected appearance and a recognizable failure. The comparison makes its claim inspectable.\r\n\r\n    Expected comparison from the published sample.     Representative failure from the same documentation.   \r\n\r\nCredit: Ed Mackey / Analytical Graphics, Inc., 2018, CC BY 4.0. [Original test README](https://github.com/KhronosGroup/glTF-Sample-Assets/blob/main/Models/AlphaBlendModeTest/README.md). These are glTF sample images, not evidence of a repaired Unreal course exhibit.\r\n\r\n   Replace “fixed it” with evidence\r\n\r\nConsider the weak note: “Fixed the texture.” It does not identify the asset, the observed problem, the change, or the verification.\r\n\r\nAn   illustrative   record can be much more useful:\r\n\r\n  Field   What to record  \r\n --- --- \r\n  Intended result   The label reads in the intended direction from the reference view.  \r\n  Symptom   Record the observed reversal and the exact asset/component.  \r\n  Hypothesis   A coordinate transformation may explain the reversal.  \r\n  Test   Compare the same image with the relevant U-coordinate operation changed.  \r\n  Change   Record the actual property, original value, and tested value.  \r\n  Evidence   Use matching views of the observed states and the relevant editor field.  \r\n  Verification   Check orientation, density/scale, the original viewing condition, and unaffected behavior.  \r\n  Reset and limits   Explain how to restore the comparison and what has not been tested.  \r\n\r\nThis table describes a record structure. It does not claim that a particular asset was repaired or establish a compulsory submission format.\r\n\r\n   Write a procedure someone can use\r\n\r\nIdentify the correct starting asset and state. Give the action where it becomes necessary. State what the reader should see after a consequential step. Use exact UI labels when they prevent ambiguity, and name the software/importer context.\r\n\r\n[Google's procedure-writing guide](https://developers.google.com/style/procedures) is a useful reference for clear steps. A source citation should resolve a concrete uncertainty, such as what an input or import setting controls.\r\n\r\n   The handoff check\r\n\r\nCan another person open the intended example, explain the comparison, repeat the verification, and reset it? Can they locate its source and dependencies? A useful record answers those questions without requiring the original author to stand beside it.\r\n\r\nUse the [exhibit-record template](exhibit-record.html) as a starting point, then remove irrelevant fields and add the details your example needs.\r\n"},{"title":"Museum investigation lab","summary":"Observe first, choose a test with a reason, and leave evidence that another person can understand.","slug":"museum-lab","group":"Practice","text":"\r\n   Pass 1: observe\r\n\r\nUse the available reference and the instructor's station list. Record the exhibit you are viewing and the exact visible difference. Include the camera, lighting, distance, pose, or interaction when it matters.\r\n\r\nThen propose a possible explanation and a useful inspection. You do not need to claim a cause that the available evidence cannot establish.\r\n\r\n  Instead of stopping at…   Add a testable observation  \r\n --- --- \r\n  “The material is broken.”   Which region differs, and how does the difference respond to light or view?  \r\n  “The UVs are wrong.”   Which part of the pattern is reversed, stretched, or displaced?  \r\n  “The model disappears.”   At what distance or viewing condition, and which representation is active?  \r\n\r\n   Look up the mechanism\r\n\r\n  Museum topic   Read this   Inspection prompt  \r\n --- --- --- \r\n  Normal response / EX24   [Normals](normals.html)   Which directions reach the shading calculation, and how are they interpreted?  \r\n  RGB/sRGB / EX23   [Texture data](textures.html)   What do the channels represent, and which interpretation matches that role?  \r\n  Coordinates / EX29   [UV lookup](uvs.html)   Is the image correct, and which coordinates read it?  \r\n  Material assignment and alpha / EXMAT   [Materials](materials.html), [handoff](handoff.html)   Are the intended faces using the correct surface definition and coverage behavior?  \r\n  Mesh detail / EXLOD   [Representations](representations.html)   Which mesh representation is selected when the difference appears?  \r\n  Geometry extension / EX20   [Mesh anatomy](meshes.html)   What structural evidence supports the diagnosis beyond the final shading?  \r\n  Skeletal displays / EXA, EX21   [Skeletal assets](skeletal.html)   Is the pose wrong, the surface following wrong, or the wrong motion playing?  \r\n\r\nThe instructor's prepared station list determines what you can edit. Some documented candidates are source-ready or display-only; their presence in the museum does not promise a supported repair exercise.\r\n\r\n   Pass 2: investigate and verify\r\n\r\nWork with the prepared group-owned editable assets. A map copy can still reference shared meshes, textures, and materials, so identify the actual asset before editing it.\r\n\r\n1. Reproduce the original symptom and keep the reference available.\r\n2. Inspect the relevant data and state a hypothesis.\r\n3. Choose a change that tests the hypothesis.\r\n4. Refresh or reimport if the workflow requires it.\r\n5. Repeat the original check under comparable conditions.\r\n6. Record the result and what remains uncertain.\r\n\r\nA source-owned fault may need a Maya edit. An engine interpretation fault may belong in Unreal. Follow the responsibility revealed by the test.\r\n\r\n   If the repair is blocked\r\n\r\nRecord the last fact you established, the missing evidence or access, and the next useful test. A precise blocked investigation remains useful. Avoid making several untracked changes simply to produce a visually improved screenshot.\r\n\r\n   Carry the work forward\r\n\r\nUse the [exhibit record](exhibit-record.html) to structure the evidence in the course's existing group-document workflow. Identify one condition that could become a [Python check](week03.html) and one observation that still needs human judgment.\r\n\r\nFor your own [museum exhibit](exhibit.html), reuse the same observation/test/verification relationship as the basis of a visitor experience.\r\n"},{"title":"Create your own museum exhibit","summary":"Make one technical idea observable through a clear comparison, a visitor question, and reproducible evidence.","slug":"exhibit","group":"Practice","text":"\r\n   Begin with what the visitor should understand\r\n\r\nComplete the sentence: “After using this exhibit, a visitor should be able to explain why…” Choose an idea small enough that the comparison can provide convincing evidence.\r\n\r\nA mirrored lookup, a normal-map shading effect, a material assignment, a coverage threshold, or a source-to-engine relationship can each support a strong exhibit. These are topic possibilities, not mandatory categories.\r\n\r\n   Design the comparison\r\n\r\n  Design question   What it helps you decide  \r\n --- --- \r\n  What stays fixed?   Controls that make the comparison interpretable.  \r\n  What changes?   The variable or relationship being investigated.  \r\n  What can a visitor observe?   The visible or measurable consequence.  \r\n  What should they predict or inspect?   The learning action, beyond pressing a button.  \r\n  What might they misunderstand?   A label, counterexample, or additional view that clarifies the mechanism.  \r\n\r\nUse the simplest asset that makes the relationship readable. Complexity should serve the explanation. More advanced work can explore a justified exception or deeper mechanism while preserving a clear beginner entry point.\r\n\r\n   Example direction: a readable label\r\n\r\nKeep the image and mesh fixed. Let the visitor compare two coordinate conditions and predict whether the label will read correctly. Show the corresponding sampling relationship and the evidence that supports the explanation.\r\n\r\nThis is a suggested concept, not a completed implementation or a claim about a particular course asset. [UV lookup](uvs.html) explains the mechanism.\r\n\r\n   Make it maintainable\r\n\r\nRecord the source, relevant asset identities, dependencies, tested settings, verification, and reset procedure. A maintainer should be able to reconstruct the intended comparison after a revision.\r\n\r\nUse a short visitor explanation and a more specific [technical record](exhibit-record.html). The [documentation examples](documentation.html) show why the expected result and recognizable failure matter.\r\n\r\n   Scope and formal requirements\r\n\r\nThe current instructor direction is to create a museum exhibit. This page develops that teaching/design direction and does not invent a rubric, deadline, group rule, submission format, or required feature list. Use the current Canvas assignment brief for those details.\r\n"},{"title":"Exhibit record template","summary":"A compact starting point for a visitor explanation and a reproducible technical record.","slug":"exhibit-record","group":"Practice","text":"\r\n   Visitor explanation\r\n\r\n-   Question:   What should the visitor predict or inspect?\r\n-   Comparison:   What changes, and what stays fixed?\r\n-   Observation:   What difference can the visitor see or measure?\r\n-   Explanation:   Why does the evidence support this mechanism?\r\n-   Explore further:   One useful concept or source link.\r\n\r\n   Maintainer record\r\n\r\n  Field   Your evidence  \r\n --- --- \r\n  Exhibit and intended result   Name/ID, purpose, reference condition.  \r\n  Source and engine assets   Actual paths or stable identities, dependencies, ownership.  \r\n  Software context   DCC, engine, relevant importer or plugin version.  \r\n  Starting state   How to reproduce the observation.  \r\n  Hypothesis and alternative   What might explain the difference.  \r\n  Test and change   The actual setting or data edit, including the prior value.  \r\n  Evidence   Matching before/after views, measured values, or the relevant editor field.  \r\n  Verification   Original failure retested and important unaffected behavior checked.  \r\n  Reset   How to restore the intended comparison and editable state.  \r\n  Limitations and next step   What is still uncertain or outside the test.  \r\n  References   Specific sources supporting the mechanism or procedure.  \r\n\r\n   A quick reader test\r\n\r\nGive the record to someone who did not watch you work. Can they find the intended asset, reproduce the comparison, and identify the result? Revise the missing information before adding more prose.\r\n\r\nThis is an adaptable documentation aid. The current assignment brief controls required deliverables. See [technical documentation](documentation.html) for a worked explanation and published visual example.\r\n"},{"title":"Reading and visual library","summary":"Choose a source by the question it answers, with practical references first and deeper theory available when it becomes useful.","slug":"resources","group":"Resources","text":"\r\n   Start here for Week 2\r\n\r\n  Resource   Read for this question   Suggested scope  \r\n --- --- --- \r\n  [Epic: Essential Material Concepts](https://dev.epicgames.com/documentation/en-us/unreal-engine/essential-unreal-engine-material-concepts?application_version=5.7)   What are the UE graph, inputs, and surface preview?   Follow one graph/preview example.  \r\n  [Scratchapixel: Basic Implementation](https://www.scratchapixel.com/lessons/3d-basic-rendering/introduction-to-texturing/introduction-to-texturing-basic-implementation.html)   How does a surface location lead to a texture lookup?   Begin with the mapping explanation and Maya UV figure; return to code later.  \r\n  [Adobe: PBR Guide, Part 1](https://www.adobe.com/learn/substance-3d-designer/web/the-pbr-guide-part-1)   What physical surface ideas motivate material controls?   Read to explain a visible surface response; defer complete model detail.  \r\n  [Maya: shading networks](https://help.autodesk.com/cloudhelp/2027/ENU/Maya-LightingShading/files/GUID-599CACDB-76C1-4FAC-AA94-0E77F0042C8D.htm)   How do texture, material, and assignment roles connect in the DCC?   Follow the node relationships.  \r\n  [Epic: FBX Material Pipeline](https://dev.epicgames.com/documentation/en-us/unreal-engine/fbx-material-pipeline-in-unreal-engine?application_version=5.7)   Which surface relationships can transfer?   Study the Maya multiple-material example and supported-conversion limits.  \r\n\r\n   Short visual examples\r\n\r\n- [Epic Material Instance chair clip](https://dev.epicgames.com/community/api/cms/videos/1_hr0fnlml/embed.html), approximately 37 seconds: predict which object changes when an exposed value changes.\r\n- [Epic Mapping Roughness clip](https://dev.epicgames.com/community/api/cms/videos/1_4goroask/embed.html), 34 seconds: watch the control and reflection together. The patterned graph is more complex than a first uniform-roughness example.\r\n- [Autodesk Maya 2027 Skin Tools](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=gJSkEyXkyHE&t=142s), suggested excerpt 2:22–3:04: compare a weight edit with the resulting bend.\r\n- [Epic blend-mode comparisons](https://dev.epicgames.com/documentation/en-us/unreal-engine/material-blend-modes-in-unreal-engine?application_version=5.7): compare stored alpha with coverage behavior.\r\n- [LearnOpenGL normal mapping](https://learnopengl.com/Advanced-Lighting/Normal-Mapping): use the direction diagram and brick comparison before exploring implementation.\r\n\r\nThe three video selections were spot-checked during the earlier media review. Source playback is separate from classroom embedding or offline availability. For study, keep a still comparison and its explanation alongside the link.\r\n\r\n   Go deeper by mechanism\r\n\r\n  Shader programming:   [The Book of Shaders](https://thebookofshaders.com/01/) introduces the idea through approachable examples; its [uniforms chapter](https://thebookofshaders.com/03/) has a live time-driven result. [LearnOpenGL: Shaders](https://learnopengl.com/Getting-started/Shaders) connects the concept to an explicit graphics-programming workflow. Their GLSL examples are conceptual/programming resources, not UE editor instructions.\r\n\r\n  Texture sampling:   [PBRT, fourth edition](https://www.pbr-book.org/4ed/Textures_and_Materials/Image_Texture) develops image filtering in depth. Use it after a UV/mip example is familiar. [Heckbert's Survey of Texture Mapping](https://publications.ri.cmu.edu/survey-of-texture-mapping) provides a historical research overview.\r\n\r\n  Surface models:   [Burley's Disney 2012 notes](https://media.disneyanimation.com/uploads/production/publication_asset/48/asset/s2012_pbs_disney_brdf_notes_v3.pdf) include parameter comparisons; start with one row rather than the whole model. [Karis's 2013 UE4 shading notes](https://blog.selfshadow.com/publications/s2013-shading-course/karis/s2013_pbs_epic_notes_v2.pdf) are historical engine-model context, not a current UE5 settings reference.\r\n\r\n  Deformation:   [Kavan et al., Skinning with Dual Quaternions](https://users.cs.utah.edu/~ladislav/kavan07skinning/kavan07skinning.html) illustrates why the deformation method can contribute to artifacts. Use the comparison first and the derivation later.\r\n\r\n   Read a source actively\r\n\r\nWrite the question before opening the reference. Identify the source's software version or model assumptions, then turn one explanation into a test on your example. A cited technique does not by itself prove that your asset uses it or that your repair worked.\r\n\r\nFor wider exploration, Bartosz Ciechanowski's [Lights and Shadows](https://ciechanow.ski/lights-and-shadows/) offers interactive explanations in a first-principles style. Its full lighting scope belongs beyond the short Week 2 introduction.\r\n"},{"title":"Working glossary","summary":"Short definitions tied to the inspection questions used in the course.","slug":"glossary","group":"Resources","text":"\r\n  Term   Working meaning  \r\n --- --- \r\n  Asset   Content and associated information used by a project. Its useful identity includes relevant dependencies.  \r\n  DCC   A digital content creation application, such as Maya.  \r\n  Engine   The application/runtime in which content is assembled and evaluated for its intended use.  \r\n  Handoff   A transfer of data, work, or responsibility between stages or people.  \r\n  Geometry   The positions and shape of a surface.  \r\n  Topology   The relationships connecting components of a mesh.  \r\n  Normal   A direction used in reasoning about a surface; distinguish face orientation and shading directions.  \r\n  Normal map   Texture-provided direction information used for shading.  \r\n  UV   A coordinate pair commonly used to locate a sample in texture space.  \r\n  Texel   A discrete element of a texture image.  \r\n  Texel density   The relationship between texture detail and surface area/size under a stated convention.  \r\n  Texture   Sampled data, often stored as channel values in an image.  \r\n  Shader   A program used in rendering calculations.  \r\n  Material   Surface logic and settings; in UE, commonly authored as a graph and asset configuration.  \r\n  Material Instance   A child asset that inherits a parent material and overrides exposed choices.  \r\n  Parameter   An exposed value or choice used by material logic or another configurable system.  \r\n  Assignment   The association of a surface definition with an object region or material slot.  \r\n  Alpha   A channel whose meaning depends on how the receiving system uses it.  \r\n  Masked   A material coverage behavior that keeps or discards samples using a cutoff.  \r\n  Two Sided   A material choice concerning backface rendering.  \r\n  sRGB   A color encoding/transfer convention; determine whether its decoding is appropriate for the data role.  \r\n  Mip   One resolution level in a texture's multiresolution representation.  \r\n  Mesh LOD   A geometry representation used at a particular level of detail.  \r\n  Streaming   Managing which needed data is currently available in memory.  \r\n  Skeleton   A hierarchy of joint transforms used in a skeletal workflow.  \r\n  Skin weight   A contribution describing how a vertex follows deformation influences.  \r\n  Bind/reference relationship   The connection between a mesh's reference state and the joint transformations used to deform it.  \r\n  Morph target   Shape-change data relying on correspondence with the base mesh.  \r\n  Metadata   Descriptive information about data, such as a declared role, source, or tag.  \r\n  Dependency   Another asset, setting, tool, or resource needed to reproduce the result.  \r\n  Validation   Comparing observed data with an explicit rule and reporting what the evidence establishes.  \r\n  Verification   Checking that a change satisfies the intended result under relevant conditions.  \r\n  Regression   A previously working behavior that fails after a change.  \r\n\r\nDefinitions here are starting points. Use the topic pages for distinctions and the software references for exact implementation behavior. Add new terms when they support a concrete example in the course, rather than turning the glossary into an unexplained vocabulary inventory.\r\n"}];
